{
  "name": "Ada",
  "tagline": "GPT-powered science explainer that answers with cited sources",
  "description": "## What it does\nAda answers any science question with a well-structured, readable article — not a chatbot reply, but a sectioned explainer with headers, prose, and inline images, generated in minutes and grounded in real sources you can open in a sidebar. Every answer is tailored to the reader: you pass an `age` and an `experience` (e.g. a 27-year-old nurse), and the model calibrates the depth, vocabulary, and framing to that audience. Crucially, Ada doesn't hallucinate citations — it can only cite from documents it actually retrieved and read during generation, so the references in the sidebar are the exact sources the article was built from.\n\n## How it works\nThe heart of Ada is an agentic, retrieval-grounded generation pipeline that runs entirely on the server (`backend/ada`). A single FastAPI endpoint, `POST /ask`, hands the question to an `AsyncCommunicator` that orchestrates OpenAI across three phases:\n\n**1. Query planning.** The question (plus the reader's age and experience) is sent to the model with a structured-output schema (`SearchTermsResponse`), which returns a short list of search terms. The original question is appended to that list so retrieval covers both the paraphrased angles and the literal ask.\n\n**2. Source retrieval → tool synthesis.** Each configured datasource pre-searches those terms and then *becomes an OpenAI function tool*. This is the core design idea: rather than dumping raw documents into the prompt, Ada runs the search first, collects the candidate resources, and exposes them to the model as a tool whose single `resource` parameter is an `enum` of the concrete things it found — specific Wikipedia sections, arXiv paper titles, article headlines, image captions. The model can only ask for resources that genuinely exist. Datasources implement a common `Datasource` / `AsyncDatasource` contract (async ones are searched concurrently with `asyncio.gather`), so new sources plug in uniformly. The shipped engines:\n- **Wikipedia** — searches pages, then parses each page's HTML with BeautifulSoup to index it into `page/section` units and extract figure images with captions; content is served per section on demand.\n- **arXiv** — finds papers via a Google Programmable Search engine scoped to arXiv, then downloads the PDF and extracts text with PyPDF2.\n- **General web** — a Programmable Search engine over the open web, with full-article scraping via newspaper3k (with a browser-UA fallback).\n- **Images** — Google image search returning captioned image URLs the model can drop into a section.\n\nGoogle Programmable Search Engine IDs back the web/arXiv/image sources; Wikipedia uses its own API.\n\n**3. Agentic drafting with enforced citations.** The model then enters a recursive tool-calling loop (`tool_choice=\"auto\"`). Each time it requests a resource, Ada fetches that content on demand, tags it with its `ref_type`, and records it in `refs_used` — the running list of everything actually cited. The loop recurses until the model stops calling tools and emits its final answer. Output is constrained to a Pydantic `GPTArticleResponse` via OpenAI structured outputs: an ordered array of sections, each with a `header`, `markdown` body, optional `image`, and a `references` list of indices into the retrieved sources. A `_verify_results` step validates the JSON and, if it's malformed, re-prompts the model to repair it before returning. The endpoint maps `refs_used` into typed `Reference` objects (Web Article / Research Paper / Image) that travel back to the client alongside the article.\n\nToken budgets are tracked with `tiktoken`, and the whole system message (which embeds the JSON schema and per-reader instructions) is guarded against blowing past the context window before any call is made.\n\n## Frontend\nThe client (`frontend/ada`) is a Create React App SPA. It's a three-part layout built with React Bootstrap: a chat-style sidebar that keeps a history of asked questions (each one selectable to re-view its answer), a main document pane that renders the returned sections with `react-markdown` (interleaving generated prose and the model-chosen images), and a slide-out off-canvas overlay that lists the citations for the selected answer as clickable, type-badged links. State flows through two React context providers (`questionProvider`, `appProvider`), and requests hit the FastAPI backend over Axios.\n\n## Stack\n**Backend:** Python, FastAPI, the OpenAI API (an `o3-mini` reasoning model driven through the structured-output / `beta.chat.completions.parse` interface with function tools), Pydantic for schemas and validation, asyncio + aiohttp for concurrent retrieval, and BeautifulSoup, PyPDF2, newspaper3k, the `wikipedia` and `arxiv` clients, and tiktoken for source ingestion and token accounting. **Frontend:** React (CRA), React Bootstrap, react-markdown + remark-gfm, and Axios. **External services:** OpenAI and Google Programmable Search.\n\n## Notable design\nThe pattern I'm most happy with is turning *retrieval results into the model's tool surface*: search happens up front, and the model is then only allowed to pick from concretely-discovered resources exposed as an enum. That makes the citation pipeline trustworthy by construction — the sidebar references are literally the documents the model pulled and read, not after-the-fact guesses — while keeping datasources fully pluggable behind one interface.",
